<!doctype html>
{#- `no-js` is the no-JavaScript contract, and it is deliberately on the element the parser sees
first. css/style.css reads it: with the class present the mobile nav is expanded and the toggle is
display:none, so the links are reachable without scripting. js/nav-init.js, loaded synchronously
below, removes it before first paint - which is what makes the collapsed header the *first* thing
painted rather than a correction applied to it (YEO-143). Removing this class from the markup
without also removing the `.no-js` rules in css/style.css ships a menu that only exists with
JavaScript. -#}
<html lang="en" class="no-js">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8" />
        <meta http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible" content="IE=edge,chrome=1" />
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />

        {# Content-Security-Policy, delivered as a meta tag because GitHub Pages serves static files
        and cannot set response headers. It has to sit this early: a meta policy governs only the
        resources the parser reaches after it. Two directives are IGNORED in meta form and are
        therefore NOT in force here, whatever the policy below might imply to a reader: *
        frame-ancestors - there is no clickjacking protection on this site. The header-only
        X-Frame-Options is equally unavailable. Any page may frame these pages. Nothing here has a
        form, a session or an authenticated state, so there is no action to trick a visitor into
        taking, but the gap is real and should not be recorded as covered. * report-uri / report-to
        - violations surface in the visitor's own console and reach us nowhere. Test pages with
        devtools open; there is no report stream to check afterwards. Closing either needs response
        headers, which needs a different host. That is a hosting decision (see the OUT OF SCOPE
        section of YEO-141), not something this tag can do. The two Cloudflare origins are not
        interchangeable: static.cloudflareinsights.com is where the analytics beacon is fetched
        from, cloudflareinsights.com is where it POSTs to. Allowing only the first loads the beacon
        and then drops every event, silently. The policy string itself lives in src/_data/site.json
        because src/redirects.njk carries the same tag and does not use this layout - two
        hand-maintained copies of a policy is exactly how one of them silently rots. #}
        <meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" content="{{ site.contentSecurityPolicy }}" />

        {#- Every search-facing string on this page is resolved here, once, from the route registry
        in src/_data/routes.js keyed by the page's own `key`. Nothing below is hand-typed per page,
        which is what stopped six pages sharing one generic title and five empty content="" tags.
        The `title`/`description` fallbacks let a page outside the registry - /404.html - supply its
        own from front matter; `page.url` backstops the canonical path for the same reason. #} {%-
        set route = routes.byKey[key] %} {%- set metaTitle = title or route.title or site.title %}
        {%- set metaDescription = description or route.description or site.description %} {%- set
        canonicalUrl = site.origin + (route.path if route else page.url) %} {%- set ogImageUrl =
        site.origin + site.ogImage.path %}

        <title>{{ metaTitle }}</title>
        <meta name="description" content="{{ metaDescription }}" />

        {#- Absolute, and in the exact trailing-slash form the sitemap uses. /bio and /bio/ are two
        URLs to a crawler, so scripts/assert-seo.js compares this string against the sitemap entry
        character for character and fails the build if they diverge. NOTE: keep angle brackets out
        of Nunjucks comments in this file - prettier parses a tag name inside one as real markup and
        the file stops parsing. #}
        <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonicalUrl }}" />
        {%- if noindex %}
        <meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow" />
        {%- endif %} {%- for author in site.authors %}
        <meta name="author" content="{{ author }}" />
        {%- endfor %}

        <meta name="theme-color" content="{{ site.themeColor }}" />

        {#- Real icon files at last. The two tags that used to sit here carried href="", which is a
        W3C error and makes every page load re-fetch its own HTML as an icon. All three are built by
        scripts/generate-icons.mjs from the single source src/icon.svg. favicon.ico is not
        vestigial: browsers request /favicon.ico by path whether or not it is declared, so omitting
        it buys a 404 on every first visit. #}
        <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32" />
        <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
        <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />

        {#- og:image must be absolute - most scrapers discard a relative one outright - and so must
        og:url, which is why both are built from site.origin rather than page-relative paths. #}
        <meta property="og:type" content="{{ route.ogType if route else 'website' }}" />
        <meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ site.title }}" />
        <meta property="og:locale" content="{{ site.locale }}" />
        <meta property="og:title" content="{{ metaTitle }}" />
        <meta property="og:description" content="{{ metaDescription }}" />
        <meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonicalUrl }}" />
        <meta property="og:image" content="{{ ogImageUrl }}" />
        <meta property="og:image:width" content="{{ site.ogImage.width }}" />
        <meta property="og:image:height" content="{{ site.ogImage.height }}" />
        <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ site.ogImage.alt }}" />

        <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
        <meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ metaTitle }}" />
        <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ metaDescription }}" />
        <meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ ogImageUrl }}" />
        <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="{{ site.ogImage.alt }}" />

        {#- WebSite on "/" and Person on "/bio/" only. src/_data/structuredData.js explains at
        length why there is deliberately no VisualArtwork markup. Already serialised there, with "<"
        escaped, so `safe` here cannot emit a script-closing sequence. #} {%- if structuredData[key]
        %}
        <script type="application/ld+json">
            {{ structuredData[key] | safe }}
        </script>
        {%- endif %} {# Orbitron and Oswald are same-origin now (YEO-141 deleted the
        fonts.googleapis.com stylesheet and the two preconnects that fed it), but a font referenced
        only from inside a stylesheet is not discovered until that stylesheet has been fetched and
        parsed - two round trips deep. These preloads are the faces that paint above the fold on
        every page: Orbitron for the site title, Oswald for the body copy. Without them,
        self-hosting is measurably *slower* on a cold cache than the CDN it replaced. crossorigin is
        required even though these are same-origin. Fonts are always fetched in CORS mode, and a
        preload whose mode does not match the real fetch does not satisfy it - the file is simply
        downloaded twice. oswald-400-latin-ext.woff2 is deliberately not preloaded: its
        unicode-range covers codepoints most pages never render, and preloading it would spend bytes
        on nothing. #}
        <link
            rel="preload"
            href="/fonts/orbitron-400-latin.woff2"
            as="font"
            type="font/woff2"
            crossorigin
        />
        <link
            rel="preload"
            href="/fonts/oswald-400-latin.woff2"
            as="font"
            type="font/woff2"
            crossorigin
        />
        {#- Render-blocking, and no `defer` - that is the entire point. It flips the header out of
        its no-JS state before the browser paints, so nothing in the header moves afterwards; with
        `defer` the collapse landed after first paint and cost CLS 0.363 on every page. It is an
        external file rather than the usual inline anti-flicker script because the CSP above sets
        `script-src 'self'` with no 'unsafe-inline', and loosening that to solve a layout problem
        would trade a real security property for a cosmetic one. Ahead of the stylesheets on
        purpose: a classic script cannot execute until every stylesheet before it has loaded, and
        the preload scanner finds the two links below regardless of what sits above them. See
        src/js/nav-init.js - it is one statement and must stay that way. #}
        <script src="/js/nav-init.js"></script>

        <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="/css/global.css" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="/css/style.css" />
    </head>

    <body>
        {# The skip link is the first focusable thing in the document, ahead of the nav every page
        repeats. It is hidden with clip-path, never display:none - display:none would take it out of
        the focus order and there would be nothing to skip with. It is absolutely positioned in both
        states, so it never displaces the sentinel below it. #}
        <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

        <!-- Zero-height sentinel: js/header.js observes it so the header's .top
         class can be toggled without a scroll listener. Must stay first in flow. -->
        <div id="top-sentinel" aria-hidden="true"></div>

        <header class="top">
            {# Exactly one h1 per page. On the homepage the site title is that h1; on every other
            page the page's own heading is, and the title degrades to a paragraph carrying the same
            .site-title styling, so the rendering is unchanged either way. Duplicated across the
            branches on purpose - prettier parses this file as HTML and cannot cope with a Nunjucks
            tag standing in for a tag name. #} {%- if key == "home" %}
            <h1 class="site-title">
                <a href="/"><span lang="zh-Hant">郭 秀 洙</span> Siew Chu Kerk</a>
            </h1>
            {%- else %}
            <p class="site-title">
                <a href="/"><span lang="zh-Hant">郭 秀 洙</span> Siew Chu Kerk</a>
            </p>
            {%- endif %} {# The toggle used to ship with the `hidden` attribute and js/nav.js
            removed it. That reveal was itself post-paint: below 48rem the button's 44px minimum is
            taller than the title's line box, so the header grew by ~19px the moment it appeared - a
            second shift on top of the nav collapse. The button is therefore always in the markup
            now, and `.no-js .nav-toggle { display: none }` in css/style.css is what hides it from a
            visitor who has no script to act on the click. #}
            <button
                type="button"
                id="mobile-nav-button"
                class="nav-toggle"
                aria-expanded="false"
                aria-controls="site-nav"
            >
                <span class="line"></span>
                <span class="line"></span>
                <span class="line"></span>
                <span class="visually-hidden">Menu</span>
            </button>
            {% include "partials/nav.njk" %}
        </header>

        {# The main landmark and the skip link's target. tabindex="-1" so the skip link actually
        moves focus here in browsers that will not focus a non-interactive element otherwise. #}
        <main id="main" tabindex="-1">{{ content | safe }}</main>

        <footer class="site-footer">
            <p>© <span lang="zh-Hant">郭 秀 洙</span> Siew Chu Kerk</p>
        </footer>

        <script src="/js/header.js" defer></script>
        <script src="/js/nav.js" defer></script>
        {%- for src in scripts %}
        <script src="{{ src }}" defer></script>
        {%- endfor %} {# Cloudflare Web Analytics. Cookieless and storing no personal data, so no
        consent banner is required or wanted - adding one would be theatre. Copied verbatim from
        YEO-141, token included; Cloudflare keys the dashboard on the token, not on a domain string,
        so there is no data-domain to get wrong. type="module" already defers - do not add a defer
        attribute and do not rewrite this as a classic script. No integrity attribute either:
        Cloudflare revises beacon.min.js in place at a stable URL and publishes no hash, so any
        value written here would be invented and would start failing the moment they ship a
        revision. Nothing on the page depends on this loading. sckerk.com is DNS-only on Cloudflare
        (grey cloud, settled in YEO-134), so the proxy will not inject the beacon for us - if this
        tag goes, measurement silently stops. #}
        <!-- Cloudflare Web Analytics -->
        <script
            type="module"
            src="https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js"
            data-cf-beacon='{"token": "719e165a84d841b1b7f0f013c99e21ac"}'
        ></script>
        <!-- End Cloudflare Web Analytics -->
    </body>
</html>
